--TEST--
Core\Jwt::verify refuses a swapped `alg`, a borrowed signature, an altered payload, another key and a shape that is not a JWT with a single sentence, and the algorithm is never selected from the token — `rule:security/protocol-roster` and `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, counted. Every library that ever accepted `alg: none` did so
// because the header chose the verifier; `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`'s first bullet removes
// the choice, and the observable consequence is that a header swap is not a
// *kind* of failure at all — it is indistinguishable from a truncated token.
// So the assertion is that six different attacks produce one sentence, which
// a member with a helpful message per failure passes on no line at all.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $other = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
string $token = Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada"], 15m, $key);
array<string> $parts = Core\Str::split($token, ".");

// `alg: none` with the signature stripped, and `alg: none` carrying the
// honest token's signature — the two shapes of the classic attack. A class
// that read the header to pick a verifier accepts the first; one that picked
// a verifier and *then* checked the tag accepts neither but reaches the
// decision through attacker-supplied text, which is what § 4 refuses.
string $none = "eyJhbGciOiJub25lIiwidHlwIjoiSldUIn0";
string $stripped = $none . "." . $parts[1] . ".";
string $borrowed = $none . "." . $parts[1] . "." . $parts[2];

// An algorithm we do not have. There is no arm for it, which is exactly why
// there is nothing to get wrong.
string $rs256 = "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9" . "." . $parts[1] . "." . $parts[2];

// The payload altered under an untouched header and signature, and a token
// whose shape was never a JWT.
string $altered = $parts[0] . "." . $parts[1] . "Z." . $parts[2];
string $shapeless = "not-a-token";

array<string> $attempts = [$stripped, $borrowed, $rs256, $altered, $shapeless, ""];
string $first = "";
int $refusals = 0;
int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($attempts as string $attempt) {
    try {
        array<tainted string> $claims = Core\Jwt::verify($attempt, $key);
        echo "a forgery verified", "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
        $refusals = $refusals + 1;
        if ($first == "") {
            $first = $refused->message;
        }
        if ($refused->message == $first) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $refusals, " refusals, ", $agreed, " of them the same sentence", "\n";

// The intact token under a key that did not sign it is the seventh, and it
// agrees too — a wrong key is not a different kind of not-authentic.
try {
    array<tainted string> $wrong = Core\Jwt::verify($token, $other);
    echo "another key verified it", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $notThisKey) {
    if ($notThisKey->message == $first) {
        echo "and a token under another key is the same sentence", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "a wrong key told the caller something extra", "\n";
    }
}
echo $first, "\n";

// Nothing above consumed the token: it still verifies under the key that
// signed it, so the six refusals are about the attempts and not about a
// member that had stopped working.
array<tainted string> $back = Core\Jwt::verify($token, $key);
if ($back["sub"] == "ada") {
    echo "the honest token still verifies", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
6 refusals, 6 of them the same sentence
and a token under another key is the same sentence
Core\Jwt::verify(): $token is not a token this key signed. Every way of not being one — a shape that is not three base64url parts, a header this class did not write, an `alg` that is not HS256, a signature under another key, and an altered payload — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
the honest token still verifies
